  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. I haven't opened the dashboard in Kibana, and I haven't checked that the rule actually runs or fires.\n\n**Fields.** The data view `kibana_sample_data_logs` has a `timestamp` field and these main fields:\n- Request: `request`, `url`, `extension`, `response` (text, with an aggregatable `response.keyword`), `referer`, `message`.\n- Traffic: `bytes`, `clientip`, `ip`.\n- Geography: `geo.src`, `geo.dest`, `geo.coordinates`.\n- Client and host: `machine.os`, `machine.ram`, `agent`, `host`.\n- Other: `memory`, `phpmemory`, `tags`.\n\n**Dashboard.** \"EFF-618bddb6 Web logs overview\" has id `794fed8c-bbe6-4f8d-a0bd-902ac758796b` and four panels:\n- Total requests (count)\n- Average bytes\n- Unique clients (unique count of `clientip`)\n- Requests over time (line chart)\n\n**Rule.** \"EFF-618bddb6 5xx spike\" has id `52dcfb90-a074-4b15-943d-7df4efd3d5ed` and uses the Elasticsearch query rule type. It runs every 1 minute. It counts documents in `kibana_sample_data_logs` whose `response.keyword` starts with \"5\" over a 5-minute window, and it fires when the count is above 10.\n\nThe rule has no actions attached, so a firing only creates an alert in Kibana and doesn't notify anyone. You didn't ask for notifications, so I left it that way.",
